{"timestamp": "2025-11-23T20:26:27.280281", "level": "INFO", "event": "heuristic_architect_completed", "data": {"problem_analysis": "The core challenge is jointly optimizing INA switch placement and worker assignments under a strict budget K, while respecting switch processing capacities, link bandwidths, and flow consistency constraints. The problem is NP-hard due to the combinatorial nature of placement and routing decisions. A key insight is that the makespan is determined by the slowest job, which depends on worker rates, aggregation bottlenecks, and network congestion. The heuristic must balance INA placement to reduce end-to-end latency without overloading switches or links, while ensuring all jobs are assigned to feasible aggregation points.", "strategy_overview": "We design a greedy, iterative improvement heuristic that first identifies high-impact candidate INA placements based on network centrality and job congestion, then assigns workers to minimize the makespan through a rate-aware routing strategy. The approach alternates between refining INA placements (considering load balancing and capacity) and reassigning workers (using a bottleneck-aware assignment rule), with periodic feasibility checks. The strategy leverages existing topology and path data to avoid recomputing paths, and uses a constructive assignment phase that respects all capacity and flow constraints.", "function_count": 5, "functions": ["_initialize_placement_candidates", "_construct_initial_placement", "_assign_workers_to_aggregation_points", "_evaluate_and_improve_placement", "solve_with_heuristic"]}}
